# World Physics
#
# Choosing which physics a world runs on, the way a designer chooses which
# subset of possible mechanics a series will use.
#
# Written for anyone building a world — a story, a series, a site, an art
# direction — who has more available rules than any coherent world can hold.
# The method is subtraction: a world is defined by what it commits to, and
# every rule you keep costs you the ones it excludes.

#>spw_world_physics
#:convention #!worldbuilding #!method #!physics
#:operation #!prime
#:fixity #!experimental
#:layer #!semantics

@registers: ~"./cauldron-registers.spw"
@material_projection: ~"./material-projection.spw"
@arrival: ~"./arrival-electrostatics.spw"
@biome: ~"./component-biome.spw"

operation = "prime"
fixity = "experimental"

expression = world[physics]{subset.commitment.consequence}<legibility>

^"the_move"{
  ~#note: "Not 'what could happen here' but 'what always happens here'."
  claim: "A world is not built by adding possibilities. It is built by choosing a small set of rules that hold everywhere in it, and declining the rest."
  ~#analogy(mechanics): `a series picks perhaps four mechanics out of hundreds available, and the look and feel follows from that subset rather than from art direction applied afterwards`
  ~#reading: `the physics is the art direction — style is what a consistent rule set looks like from outside`
  ~#antipattern: "adding a rule because a scene needs it. A rule introduced once is a special case; a rule that holds everywhere is physics."
}

^"the_questionnaire"{
  ~#note: "Four questions per rule. Borrowed from @registers, where each coordinate has to answer the same four before it can compose with anything."
  carrier: .{
    asks = `what physically carries this?`
    ~#why: `a rule with no carrier cannot be observed, and an unobservable rule is a claim about the author rather than about the world`
    example = `charge needs something charged; grief needs something that remembers`
  }[reg=facet]
  ordering: .{
    asks = `is distance between two values meaningful, or only difference?`
    ~#why: `this decides whether your world supports gradual change or only category change, and almost every pacing question follows from it`
    ordered = `more/less — heat, debt, exhaustion, altitude`
    categorical = `different in kind — element, house, species, tongue`
    ~#rule: `never compute a distance in a categorical rule; two houses are not two units apart`
  }[reg=facet]
  identity: .{
    asks = `what value of this changes nothing?`
    ~#why: `a rule with no identity cannot accumulate, so nothing in the world can be spent or saved along it`
    ~#consequence: `if you cannot name the zero, you do not yet have a quantity — you have a mood`
  }[reg=facet]
  composition: .{
    asks = `when two of these meet, what results?`
    options = #[`sum`, `strongest wins`, `least settled wins`, `span`, `plurality`, `annihilate`, `refuse to combine`][reg=set]
    ~#why: `this is the rule readers actually learn, because it is the one they can predict with`
    ~#note(refusing is a choice): `two things that cannot combine is a physics, and often a more interesting one than a sum`
  }[reg=facet]
}

^"worked_example"{
  ~#note: "This site, stated as a world, so the method is checkable against something real."
  chosen: #[
    `charge — signed, sums, identity 0. Some acts credit a reader, some spend`,
    `liminality — ordered ladder, composes as a span. Distance from arrival is meaningful`,
    `saturation — a solution holds only so much of one thing before more stops dissolving`,
    `phase — categorical, six values, composes as most-tangible-present`
  ][reg=set]
  declined: #[
    `time of day — no carrier here, and no consequence would follow from it`,
    `alignment — categorical with no composition rule anyone could predict with`,
    `scarcity — would make gathering competitive, which contradicts charge`
  ][reg=set]
  ~#reading: `four rules, and the look follows: things settle at different rates because their charge differs, and a page feels crossed because the ladder has distance`
  ~#test: "a rule earns its place when removing it would change what a reader can predict. If nothing breaks, it was decoration."
}

^"for_image_work"{
  ~#note: "Style development grounded in the physics a world needs, rather than in reference-chasing."
  ~#claim: "A recognisable style is usually a physics commitment held long enough to become visible — how light behaves, what has weight, what edges do when they meet."
  method: #[
    `name the two or three physical facts every image must obey`,
    `state what they do when they meet, before generating anything`,
    `treat an image that violates them as evidence about the prompt, not as a happy accident to keep`
  ][reg=set]
  ~#why(uncanniness is information): "the specific way a model's output feels wrong is a readout of which physics it did not commit to — a face that is almost right usually has inconsistent light, not inconsistent anatomy"
  ~#reading: `that discrimination comes from making rather than from viewing, which is why it develops in people who generate a lot and stays absent in people who only look`
}

#>wonder_world_physics_1
?["Which rules here would a reader miss if they were removed?"]{
  #:depth #!educational ~#lens(arc)
  ~#tension: "a world can carry rules nobody predicts with. Those cost consistency work and return nothing, and they are invisible from inside because the author remembers why each one is there."
  ~#hypothesis: "a rule is load-bearing when a reader can use it to guess right about a scene they have not been shown"
  !probe{ "state each rule of a world, then describe an unseen scene using only those rules. If someone who knows the world can predict what happens, the set is doing work; if they cannot, the rules are decoration with a vocabulary." }
  $%[rule.carrier, rule.composition, reader.prediction]
}

^"validation"{
  probe: `every rule named in a world states carrier, ordering, identity and composition`
  falsification: "a rule is introduced for one scene and never governs another"
  ~#note(honest limit): "this method produces coherence, not interest. A perfectly consistent world with four boring rules is boring on schedule. Choosing which physics is still taste; this only stops the set from contradicting itself."
}
